{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>With you</b> — they will volunteer freely on your behalf. Rabbi Moses said: it is the breaking of dawn, and the word <i>rechem</i> is a metaphor, like <b>then he breaks forth like the dawn</b>. He praises David and likens the moisture in the body to dew. The meaning: because his moisture did not fail in the many wars he fought, and so from the breaking of dawn for you the moisture of your youth remains. Others said: from the low, dark place, yours is the portion of your youth. And <b>tal</b> means as in <b>he shall cast the lot</b>. And Rabbi Yeshu'ah said: for from your mother's womb you were prepared to be king. And what seems correct to me: that <b>nedavot</b> is an abbreviated form, like <b>the rain of freewill offerings</b>; the meaning is, if you needed to wage war, they will come to you by day like the rain of freewill offerings, and you will see them in holy splendor, <b>from the womb, from the dawn</b>: from the time the sun rises it will come like the dew that you were accustomed to see in your youth when you were tending sheep."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 110, :notes {0 "Rabbi Moses and Rabbi Yeshu'ah identified here by name only; the Gaon and other standard epithets are absent. The phrase עלות השחר rendered literally as 'the sun rises' though it may refer more precisely to dawn; preserved Ibn Ezra's compression throughout."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:39:23.943900433Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}